For more up-to-date information, please check the web-site:
http://Lyntin.sourceforge.net/



* WHY SHOULD YOU USE LYNTIN?

Lyntin is: 

	1. a featureful and highly extensible mud client
	2. a bot/agent development environment
	3. based on Tintin++ functionality--tried and true

Lyntin is written entirely in Python--a nicely written and very portable
programming language.  Thusly Lyntin is very platform independent (minus
a few issues here and there I'm sure).  This also means that your 
actions (or triggers, or autos, or whatever you call them) are no longer
restricted to simple 'if you see this then send this' kinds of things.
They can be Python functions which do anything from setting a variable
to forking a web spider.  Anything Python allows (which is a lot),
Lyntin allows.  In addition, your code can interface with Lyntin's
code.  

Lyntin is great if:

	1. you're at work and you want to telnet/ssh to a server and
	   then mud from there without anyone knowing
	2. you want a mud client that you can see the source code to
	   and adjust it to suit your needs
	3. you want a mud client that has a sophisticated API for 
	   enhancing and building bots/agents/triggers more advanced than
	   "if you see this then do this"
	4. you want a mud client that works on all your machines 
	   and has a text ui and a gui

Lyntin is not great if:

	1. you prefer wizards to the command line
	2. you hate Python



* BUG REPORTS, QUESTIONS, COMMENTS, CURSES?

Lyntin was originally written by Lyn Headley, laheadle@cs.uchicago.edu .
Lyntin is currently being maintained by Will Guaraldi, 
willhelm@users.sourceforge.net .  (as of 1.3.2)

We appreciate ALL types of feedback.

Hell, if you even USE this, drop us a line on the mailing list:

	lyntin-devl@lists.sourceforge.net

All bugs, feature-requests, and other things should be reported
at: 

	http://sourceforge.net/projects/lyntin/

If possible, when noting bugs, please do a "#diagnostics" and include
the OS/Python information at the bottom as well as any stack traces
and pertinent information.  The more information you include, the
more we have to go by to understand what the problem is.



* HOW TO CONTRIBUTE

Development and maintenance is currently entirely done by the maintainer.
If you're interested in sending in bug fixes, please feel welcome.  I'm
extremely accomodating to most methods of sending in patches.

A diff -u output file is great for this sort of thing.

All patches and such things should be sent to the mailing list at:

	lyntin-devl@lists.sourceforge.net



* OTHER CONCERNS

The latest release of Lyntin is always available from:

	http://lyntin.sourceforge.net/

Lyntin is still under development, and there aren't many testers, so
we apologize profusely for any bugs you may encounter and such.

In general, work on Lyntin progresses as time allows.  We do respond 
to emails on the mailing list with a day or so.  All issues are tracked
via the bug-tracker and task-tracker on our SourceForge project site.
If you have problems and fix them, by all means send us patches and
we'll happily work with them and credit you.



* LYNTIN vs. TINTIN++

Lyntin has many of the same features as Tintin++ and the commands
are based on Tintin++.  However, there are a series of Tintin++
commands we do not currently support:

^<<
  map           mark          path
  pathdir       snoop         split
  tickset       unsplit       unpath
  suspend       system
^>>


* DOCUMENTATION 

Lyntin incorporates the 'look and feel' of Tintin++, so it should be
pretty easy to make the transition.  It offers all the major features
of Tintin++, including multiple sessions, but leaves a few minor things
out.

There are "in-game" help files covering all the commands and most 
other things we could think of which can be accessed with the #help 
command.

If you find any bugs in the documentation or want to contribute
documentation, please feel free to send such things to the mailing
list.



* ERRATA

That should be about it.  In-game help is accessed by typing #help.
When you start Lyntin for the first time, type "#help general"
and that'll get you started.  Read through the various help files
at your leisure.

Enjoy!

the Lyntin development folks
http://lyntin.sourceforge.net/
